    <DataNode elementId="AT1G22940-MONOMER" textLabel="Thiamine biosynthetic bifunctional enzyme TH1, chloroplastic [Includes: Thiamine-phosphate synthase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q5M731" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE, dreher">The TH1 gene encodes a multifunctional, chloroplast-localized protein capable of catalyzing several sequential steps in thiamine (vitamin B1) biosynthesis |CITS: [17174261]|. First, it leads to the production of |FRAME:AMINO-HYDROXYMETHYL-METHYLPYRIMIDINE-PP| (HMP-PP) the pyrimidine heterocyclic subunit of thiamine. Then it catalyzes the condensation of HMP-PP and |FRAME:THZ-P| (HET-P) to form |FRAME: THIAMINE-P| (TMP) |CITS: [17174261] [16666289]|. TH1 can also phosphorylate |FRAME:HMP| (HMP), to generate HMP-P |CITS: [17174261]|, but the biological relevance of this reaction remains unknown because the THIC enzyme can produce HMP-P directly from the |FRAME: 5-PHOSPHORIBOSYL-5-AMINOIMIDAZOLE| (AIR) precursor |CITS:[15326535]|. &lt;i&gt; th1&lt;/i&gt; mutants are thiamine auxotrophs that remain incapable of producing TMP even when supplied with exogenous HMP-PP or HET-P |CITS: [16666289], [17174261]|. Because vitamin B1 is a required cofactor for many enzymes involved in primary metabolism, TH1, a single copy enzyme, is essential for viability. TH1 in Arabidopsis and other plants |CITS:[9700068]| differs from related biosynthetic enzymes in &lt;i&gt;E.coli&lt;/i&gt; and yeast. Three different proteins provide the comparable kinase (PdxK, ThiD) and pyrophosphorylase (ThiE) activities in E.coli (rev. in |CITS:[10382260]|). Meanwhile, &lt;i&gt;S. cerevisiae&lt;/i&gt; has a bifunctional enzyme, but, instead of combining an N-terminal HMP-P kinase and with a C-terminal TMPPase activity like TH1 |CITS:[9700068]|, the yeast enzyme (Thi6) brings together an N-terminal TMPPase activity with a C-terminal HET kinase activity (rev in |CITS:[16826377]|). TMPPase activity was first associated with a ~100 kD peak isolated purified from Arabidopsis extracts using gel filtration |CITS: [16666289]|, but the predicted molecular weight of the TH1 monomer is ~56 kD. Studies on the &lt;i&gt;Zea mays&lt;/i&gt; homolog &lt;i&gt;thi3&lt;/i&gt; suggest that it may exist as a dimer |CITS: [17696876]|. Further experiments will be needed to determine whether TH1 is enzymatically active as a monomer or part of a complex.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G22940-MONOMER" />
